qbone (1.19.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * The image is 429 MB smaller. The free space it ships with was added on top
    of what the shrink arrived at, and the shrink cannot pack a filesystem down
    to what it holds: metadata it will not relocate sets a floor, and on the
    last release that floor already left 684 MB free before the 400 MB margin
    went on. Taking the margin as a floor on free space instead leaves the
    promise where it was and writes 429 MB less to every card, which is a
    minute or so off every write on a slow one.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans@huebner.org>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 14:02:59 +0200

qbone (1.18.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * A setup file may carry several ssh keys, as the authorized_keys file it is
    named after does: somebody with a workstation and a laptop has two. Every
    line is checked before any of them is written, so a file with one bad line
    among four changes nothing.
  * A setup file may say what address the machine takes - address with its
    prefix, router, and the servers to ask - and saying nothing about it is what
    leaves a machine on DHCP, so the usual case has no setting and nothing to
    get wrong. An address with no prefix, or a router with no address beside it,
    is refused while the SD card is still in somebody's hand rather than after a
    boot nobody can reach. The address is written beside the bridge's own
    configuration rather than into it, so the provisioning tool copying its
    files back does not put the machine on DHCP again; removing that one file is
    what returns it to DHCP.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 19:15:00 +0200

qbone (1.17.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * A QUniLator carries one operator: a user name and a password together, which
    open the web interface, the SMB, FTP and SFTP shares of the image library
    and an ssh login alike. The name is half of the credential, so the right
    password under another name is refused, and the service account that owns
    the image tree takes no login of its own. An installation whose stored
    password carries no user name is one from before this, and comes up asking
    to be set up, saying so in the journal; the first-run dialog settles it in
    the same breath as the host name and an ssh key.
  * A card can carry its setup, so a QUniLator is ready the first time it boots.
    qunilator.toml on the card's FAT partition names the operator, the host
    name and an ssh key; the first boot applies it and deletes it. The password
    stands in the file, or in the three shapes it is checked in - a PBKDF2
    digest for the web interface, a crypt(3) hash for the Linux account, an NT
    hash for the file shares - so a tool preparing a card need not put the
    password on it. The same file written later is the way back in when a
    password is lost.
  * "<name> --setup-operator" creates that account from a command line, reading
    the password from stdin, and "--adopt-account" makes an account that was
    there before the operator's own, keeping its home, its files and its shell.
  * A debug panel shows what the processor holds and what is in memory:
    registers read from a halted CPU, memory dumped with the words nothing
    answered marked as such, and disassembly of either kind of machine, since
    the card is bus master and asks the processor nothing. As many views as the
    work needs, held in the URL so a screen comes back on reload.
  * A QUniLator comes up dark. The configuration the switches name is loaded
    without being put on the bus, because a card is fitted to a machine and
    configured afterwards, and what it carries may describe a backplane it is no
    longer in. A configuration that starts itself says so afterwards, in the
    journal and in a standing notice, and asking for that is a question the
    interface puts before it is set. An apply now says what it did as well as
    what it refused.
  * An empty RL drive slot answers with no status lines. Drive Ready and Drive
    Error are lines a drive asserts over the cable, so a select field naming a
    slot with nothing in it reads clean and a function addressed there ends in
    Operation Incomplete - which is how a program finds out which drives a
    machine carries. CZRLG reads 001200 where it expects it.
  * A read past the top of the address space is answered rather than fatal.
  * On UNIBUS the emulated processors are always present, and enabling one takes
    the bus.
  * The manual is published at qunilator.com: a page for each screen of the web
    interface, walkthroughs that were each run on hardware first, a page for
    operators coming from QUniBone Classic, and a glossary that settles what the
    four names mean.
  * Each release image is published with its SHA-256 beside it, so a download
    can be checked before it is written to a card.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 16:30:00 +0200

qbone (1.16.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * An image file's mode is the medium's write ring. Clearing a file's write
    permission write-protects the image it holds, the way pulling a tape's ring
    does: the emulator reads the mode as the cartridge goes into the drive
    rather than letting the open decide, so "chmod 444" protects an image from a
    service that runs as root. An image the operator has protected keeps its
    protection when the machine releases it.
  * The System page says which UART carries the Linux login. Each of the three
    is either a login or the emulator's, one login is kept at all times, and a
    port an enabled DL11 or the console bridge holds is refused a login and
    names what holds it. The kernel console follows the first port with a login,
    so a boot message never lands in a line an emulated device has. The 115200
    login drop-in ships for all three ports.
  * The shared onboarding password of the base image is locked once the operator
    has an account of their own, so the credentials the setup dialog takes are
    the only ones that reach the board.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 09:05:00 +0200

qbone (1.15.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * A TS11/TSV05 tape subsystem answers on the bus. The reel turns after a
    rewind answers, an erase blanks the tape ahead and motion into the blank
    ends incomplete, BOT is recognised at both the places it lives, and the
    revision-level field reports the module's switches and the selected unit.
    DEC's own diagnostics run against it, CVTSD and CVTSC reporting no errors,
    and 2.11BSD drives it through a kernel configuration whose tape is the TS11.
  * A console scripting harness drives a machine from a script: input is paced
    on the guest's echo, a script brings its own machine up, a stuck step fails
    at the prompt rather than at the deadline, and a session is recorded and
    rendered for a reader. Console sessions and an XXDP runner built on them are
    offered over MCP.
  * The media manager is a tree an image can be dragged through, a machine is a
    file rather than something baked into the image, and a configuration is
    carried off the board and brought back. The sample pack ships in dl/.
  * A packaged ROM is copied into the media tree rather than named by path, a
    device parameter that names a file is believed, and an M9312 boot address
    names a label the ROMs define or is refused.
  * A memory card is placed by start address and size, the top of the address
    space belongs to the CPU module, a probe reports the machine's memory rather
    than the board's own, and a card goes in without a walk of the address
    space. The cards the PRU answers for carry an access lamp per direction.
  * The USB port carries a network interface and a login. A CDC Ethernet and CDC
    ACM gadget is built over configfs, usb0 holds 192.168.7.2 whether or not a
    host is attached and serves the attached host a lease, and a getty answers
    on the gadget's serial port. The login banner names both addresses the board
    answers on, and a serial login answers on ttyS1 beside the debug header.
  * The Add-a-device list scrolls, sorts and searches, a refused device opens a
    dialog saying why, attaching a medium puts the drive in the machine, an RL
    medium changes without spinning the pack down first, and a power cycle
    rebuilds every card.
  * The setup dialog names the board and reaches its shell, sets a user name
    alongside the password, and says when it is working. One program drives the
    board at a time, and the interactive program is <name>-cli.
  * build.env names the board and the bus it carries and stops a build that is
    half-filled, crossbuild.sh -g builds unoptimised with debug symbols, the CPU
    core tests run in the crossbuild, and a deploy reaches a board flashed from
    the release image. The PRU firmware builds inside the container.
  * A busy DMA slot refuses the transfer rather than aborting, DMA runs under the
    internal-bus firmware, and the DMR holdoff expires by distance from its
    arming.

 -- Hans Huebner <hans.huebner@gmail.com>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 08:00:00 +0200

qbone (1.14.0-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * An MRV11-D universal PROM module sits on the bus with the MXV11-B2 bootstrap
    set DEC supplied for it in its sockets, so a machine whose backplane carries
    no bootstrap boots one. The sockets take a ROM image or a MACRO-11 listing
    from the media tree instead, the array answers through the module's two
    bootstrap windows or as a range in memory space, and the dashboard carries
    the module's card beside the memory map.
  * An interrupt acknowledge is answered whatever the bus is doing, and a queued
    DMA waits for the processor to finish entering its interrupt before it takes
    the bus. 2.11BSD runs steadily on that, and a dump of /usr to tape completes.
  * MSCP initialization converges under a host that re-initializes the port back
    to back, the step-1 code is posted while the host is still looking for it, an
    interrupt follows every ring transition the host armed, and GET UNIT STATUS
    answers with the unit's real flags and walks on to the next unit. The
    MXV11-B2 bootstrap boots an MSCP disk on this.
  * The emulated VAX-11/780 runs on emulated time with its clock's instruction
    rate pinned to it, so VMS calibrates its delay loops and boots reliably; a
    byte write reaches the half of the bus its address selects, and the run
    controls and the front-panel state reach the processor.
  * The DZ carries eight lines on the UNIBUS and rings an arriving call, and the
    guest answers the ring before the line carries. A mux line listens on DTR
    while it can take a caller, several lines share one listen port, and the
    dashboard orders each line's lamps by the pairs the signals work in. The
    DHV11 has a panel of its own.
  * A machine switched on from the web interface starts its processor with a
    clear console, a freshly loaded configuration comes up switched off, and
    loading one lands on the dashboard. The console keeps the keyboard, its text
    can be selected, it follows a change of the emulated machine and it answers a
    guest's terminal-identification query.
  * The image library makes a blank medium to write on, disk or tape.
  * A processor's bus access is timed and a stalled cycle is named, so what a
    machine spends on the bus can be read off the board.

